>> title: Pilau Rice
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: rice, indian, basmati, saffron, side
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: pilau, pulao, pillau rice, pullao
>> pairs-with: korma, madras, bhuna
>> servings: 6
>> time: 1 hr
>> step.1: rinse, then soak 30 min
>> step.2: fry 1 min, until the cardamom swells
>> step.3: toast 2 min, until the grains turn chalky
>> step.4: simmer 12 min covered, then rest 10 min off the heat

Soak @basmati rice{2%cup}(400 g) in @cold water{4%cup}(950 mL) ~soak{30%min}, having first rinsed it in four or five changes of water until they run clear, then drain it in a sieve.

Fry @green cardamom{5%pods}(bruised), @cloves{4}, @cinnamon{1%stick}(2-in piece), @bay leaf{1} and @cumin seeds{1%tsp} in @ghee{3%Tbs}(42 g) in a #heavy pot{} with a tight lid ~fry{1%min}.

Toast @&(~2)drained rice{} in @&(~1)spiced ghee{} with @ground turmeric{1/2%tsp} ~toast{2%min}, turning it with a flat spoon rather than stirring, until the grains look chalky and are coated in fat.

Simmer @&(~1)rice{} with @water{3%cup}(710 mL) and @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} under a tight lid ~simmer{12%min} on the lowest heat, then take it off and ~rest{10%min} without lifting the lid, and fork it up from the edge in.

This is the rice that comes with everything, and it is not the tiffin counter's lemon rice. Two things make it: soaking, which lets the grain take up water before it ever meets heat so it lengthens instead of splitting, and the rest at the end, which lets the steam finish the job and the grains firm up enough to separate. Lifting the lid to look is how a pilau becomes a risotto. The turmeric is for colour and the shops use a pinch of saffron or a few drops of food colour dropped in at the rest, streaked rather than mixed.
